<file path="docs/bugs/elevator-enemy-queueing.md">
# Elevator Crowding During Enemy Traversal

- **Status:** Open; deferred after M1C navigation acceptance.
- **First observed:** Manual M1C navigation review on 2026-09-08.
- **Area:** Multi-enemy elevator traversal, local body collision, and route
  execution.
- **Impact:** Enemies can crowd a small elevator while attempting upward
  traversal, so typically only one rider completes the trip per cycle. The
  single-rider route remains functional.

## Reported symptom

Several enemies converge on the same elevator staging/deck area while pursuing
across floors. Their ordinary body collision makes the crowd visibly bunch up,
and the small platform normally admits one enemy at a time. The remaining
enemies can delay one another instead of forming a readable queue.

This is not a cross-floor clairvoyance, replay, or single-rider traversal
failure. M1C intentionally leaves reservations and passenger ownership out of
scope, so the current behavior is an accepted limitation rather than a reason
to reopen the completed milestone.

## Expected future behavior

A future traversal/crowd slice should make upward elevator use legible and
reliable for multiple enemies. Candidate directions include a larger authored
platform or a bounded queue policy that gives a rider already on the deck
priority while other enemies stage and dequeue in a stable order. The design
must preserve autonomous elevator timing: AI may observe and wait, but may not
call, cycle, teleport, or acquire scripted passenger ownership.

Downward traversal deserves separate evaluation. The desired feel is that an
enemy can continue its hunt by taking an ordinary, physically valid downward
opening more easily than it can board a crowded upward platform. Whether that
means using an existing open elevator aperture, falling, or another authored
descent rule is not yet specified; it must retain the established aperture,
support, and fall authority rather than adding a special-case teleport.

## Current reproduction record

1. Start `?arena=navigation` and allow multiple enemies to pursue toward an
   upper-floor elevator.
2. Let them converge at the same staging/deck area while the autonomous lift
   cycles.
3. Observe crowding and that one rider normally boards while others remain
   blocked or delayed.

Canvas/Three.js parity, exact enemy count, elevator phase, and a minimized
route have not yet been recorded. This report intentionally does not prescribe
a fix.

## Acceptance boundary for a future repair

- Multiple enemies form a bounded, deterministic waiting/rider policy or use a
  deliberately enlarged authored deck.
- A current rider retains priority without changing the autonomous elevator
  schedule.
- Missed cycles, topology edits, death, body collision, replay, reset, and
  pool swap-removal remain deterministic.
- Legitimate downward traversal is tested separately from upward queueing.
- Single-rider M1C behavior and the prohibition on cross-floor clairvoyance
  remain intact.
</file>

<file path="docs/bugs/enemy-health-bar-through-wall.md">
# Enemy Health Bar Visible Through Solid Walls

- **Status:** Open; investigation and repair intentionally deferred.
- **First observed:** During Lantern 0.7.0 review, human visual report on 2026-07-27.
- **Suspected origin:** The 0.6.x health-bar presentation; earliest affected version is unconfirmed.
- **Area:** Actor health-bar visibility and Three.js presentation.
- **Impact:** Presentation information leak; no simulation, replay, or schema failure has been observed.
- **Renderer/backend:** Not captured in the original report.
- **Product direction:** World-space enemy health bars are likely prototype/debug presentation and may not ship in the final game.

## Reported symptom

While the player was inside the closed room on the test map, an enemy health bar was visible through the solid wall. This can reveal an enemy's presence and health when its presentation should be occluded or concealed with the owning actor. The issue was only noticed during 0.7.0 review, but the reporter suspects it relates to the health-bar work introduced in 0.6.x rather than the tactical-AI changes.

## Expected behavior

An enemy health bar is subordinate to that enemy's visibility. A solid wall between the player and enemy should prevent the bar from appearing through the wall, and the bar should reveal or conceal together with its actor through TrueSight transitions.

## Current reproduction record

The report has not yet been independently reproduced or minimized. The best available steps are:

1. Run Lantern 0.7.0 using the same 3D route and backend as the original observation.
2. Enter the closed room on the test map while an enemy remains outside on the other side of a solid wall.
3. Look toward the wall as the enemy moves nearby.
4. Observe whether the enemy health bar appears through the wall while the enemy should be occluded.

The exact renderer route, backend, device, DPR, camera angle, enemy position, enemy health, frequency, and TrueSight diagnostic state were not recorded. Canvas2D has not been assessed for this report.

## Investigation notes

No root cause or introducing release is assigned yet. Health bars were introduced in 0.6.0, and Lantern 0.6.1 deliberately left their depth testing and depth writing disabled while placing the fill and track in one transparent render queue; that repaired the black-fill compositing defect. The new symptom may involve that depth state, the screen-right bar offset, instance visibility, or TrueSight opacity/mask sampling, but those are hypotheses only. Reproducing against both 0.6.0 and 0.6.1 is required before attributing the regression.

A future investigation should capture:

- the exact `?renderer=3d` or `?renderer=3d&backend=webgl` route, device, DPR, and camera position;
- the enemy ID, position, health, snapshot visibility data, and `window.__lantern.isVisible(...)` result;
- actor, track, and fill instance opacity across the reveal/conceal transition;
- whether wall depth alone, TrueSight alone, or their interaction permits the leak;
- Canvas2D behavior as the renderer-independent regression oracle.

## Acceptance boundary for a future repair

Future triage has two valid dispositions: repair occlusion if world-space enemy health bars are retained, or remove the bars from the relevant final-game presentation and verify that no replacement leaks enemy information through walls. No decision is required while the current prototype bars remain deferred.

- An enemy and both resident health-bar meshes remain concealed behind the closed-room wall in automatic 3D and forced WebGL 2.
- The bar reveals and fades with its owning actor, including partial TrueSight transitions and the screen-right offset.
- Green, amber, and red fills remain visible above the dark track when the actor is legitimately visible; the 0.6.1 black-fill defect must not return.
- Canvas2D/Three.js visibility parity, resident resource identity, and the existing light/material topology remain intact.
- Simulation visibility, AI knowledge, replay behavior, and schemas remain unchanged.
</file>

<file path="docs/bugs/navigation-floor-debug-and-ai.md">
# Navigation floor transitions: stale overlay and suspected AI retargeting

- **Status:** Open playtest reports; not independently reproduced.
- **Priority:** Near-term investigation before substantial map authoring.
- **Reported:** 2026-09-15, during the transition to saved JSON arena loading.
- **Route:** `?arena=navigation&renderer=3d`, developer tools/navigation view open.
- **Context:** Ground-to-second-floor elevator travel. Exact seed, enemy IDs,
  AI state/evidence, selected debug tool, and map revision were not captured.
  The saved navigation map now permits a single urchin to simplify reproduction.

## NAV-001: Navigation overlay stays on the previous floor

After reaching the second floor, the visible navigation graph does not update
until the player enters/clicks the editor. Refreshing the editor then displays
the graph for the new floor.

Expected: in play mode, the debug graph follows the player's viewed runtime
floor automatically. In edit mode, it follows the selected editor floor.
Opening the editor should not be needed to refresh a play-mode overlay.

Investigation lead: `createNavigationTopologyView()` currently chooses its layer
from `editor.activeLayerId`, and `src/main.js` supplies the editor view in play
mode too. Verify that relationship during a runtime floor handoff; this is a
code observation, not a completed diagnosis or proof that AI state changes.

## NAV-002: Enemies appear to redirect upstairs when the player changes floors

Ground-floor enemies appear to change navigation and head toward the elevator
after the player reaches the second floor. Brief yellow route/goal lines flash
on the grid, and enemies appear to seek a destination they cannot reach directly.
The player suspects the AI is using the viewed floor's navigation graph.

Expected: changing the camera/editor floor cannot retarget an enemy. Each enemy
keeps its own runtime floor and home; cross-floor pursuit must follow the
existing evidence rules and a valid route. A witnessed elevator pursuit may be
correct, so distinguish it from unintended patrol/home changes.

Reproduce with one enemy and compare a player elevator trip with an editor-only
layer change. Capture enemy floor, home floor, perception state, pursuit
evidence, route intent, local goal, and elevator phase before/after each. Include
a trip the enemy cannot witness. Check the overlay independently: a stale or
misfiltered route drawing does not by itself establish a simulation defect.

## Debug readability follow-up

The light-blue triangular graph, purple/pink markers, and transient yellow
lines are difficult to interpret without a legend. Explain authored links,
patrol nodes, elevator endpoints, selected enemy route, and local goal in the
debug UI. Keep this presentation improvement separate from the two behavioral
reports above. Record the active overlay during reproduction because AI View
has additional colors beyond the authored navigation graph.

Acceptance: play-mode floor changes refresh the correct overlay without an
editor toggle; editor-only layer changes leave enemy behavior unchanged; and
cross-floor enemy travel is explained by recorded pursuit/home intent rather
than the debug view's active floor. Verify in a real browser as well as tests.
</file>

<file path="docs/bugs/obelisk-activation-readability.md">
# Obelisk activation range is difficult to distinguish from occlusion

- **Status:** Open; deferred diagnostics/readability bug.
- **Priority:** Polish before encounter-heavy map authoring.
- **Reported:** 2026-09-16.
- **Route:** `?renderer=3d&arena=navigation-testing`.

## Symptom

An obelisk configured with a 20-meter activation radius appears during play to
have roughly a 10-meter radius. The one-meter editor grid makes the apparent
discrepancy especially noticeable.

The distance calculation is currently correct. In `navigation-testing`, the
player start at `(3.5, 18.5)` is about `17.03m` from the obelisk at
`(20.5, 19.5)`, but intervening solid cells block the separate line-of-sight
requirement. Because the UI exposes neither test, the first position where
line-of-sight opens can be mistaken for the range boundary.

## Expected correction

Keep the current sentry rules unless playtesting changes their design: same
floor, no more than 20 meters away, and unobstructed map line-of-sight. Add
detached, bounded obelisk diagnostics to the stable-identity inspector or an
appropriate encounter debug view. At minimum, expose:

- measured player distance and configured activation radius;
- same-floor result;
- in-range result;
- line-of-sight result; and
- a concise dormant reason such as `different-floor`, `out-of-range`,
  `occluded`, `capped`, or `waiting-for-cadence`.

The diagnostics must observe simulation truth without moving spawn authority
into the DOM or renderer. Canvas2D and Three.js should communicate the same
reason, and the final acceptance check should reproduce the original
navigation-testing route in a real browser.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.1.0-blast-physics.md">
# Project Lantern — 0.1.0: Blast Physics

> **Version:** 0.1.0 (historical milestone M0.1)
>
> **Status:** implemented supplement to M0
> **Runtime:** browser JavaScript ES modules
> **Purpose:** add inspectable explosion force and a minimal set of solid dynamic bodies without introducing a general ECS, damage system, or particle physics.

The original M0 contract remains the historical simulation foundation. This document owns the M0.1 additions and intentional changes.

## 1. Player-visible behavior

- A fireball explodes on the first solid map cell or rock it hits.
- The same explosion event produces the visual particle burst and gameplay impulse. Particles do not create or carry force.
- The player, including the caster, and all rocks inside the blast radius are candidates for knockback.
- A solid cell between the explosion origin and a body blocks that body's blast impulse.
- Rocks collide with map walls, the player, and other rocks.
- The debug renderer shows blast radius, blast rays, blocked rays, response vectors, body contacts, mass, and separate player external velocity.

Fireball lifetime is 4 seconds in M0.1 so a missed shot in the bounded 24m arena reaches a wall instead of disappearing in open floor.

## 2. Units and body properties

The simulation continues to use meters, seconds, kilograms, and X/Z ground-plane motion.

```text
player radius = 0.30 m
player mass   = 75 kg

rock density = 2,600 kg/m3
rock mass    = (4/3) * pi * radius^3 * density
```

| Archetype | Radius | Approximate mass |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| small | 0.10 m | 10.9 kg |
| medium | 0.30 m | 294 kg |
| large | 0.90 m | 7,940 kg |

These are intentionally physical defaults. The blast constant is the gameplay tuning control; rock masses are not made artificially similar just to equalize motion.

## 3. Explosion impulse

Default explosion parameters:

```text
blast radius       = 2.5 m
pressure impulse   = 800 N*s/m2
application        = instantaneous
map occlusion      = binary, solid cells block
dynamic occlusion  = none
```

For each candidate body:

```text
centerDistance  = length(bodyCenter - explosionOrigin)
surfaceDistance = max(0, centerDistance - bodyRadius)
t               = clamp(surfaceDistance / blastRadius, 0, 1)
falloff         = 1 - smoothstep(t)
projectedArea   = pi * bodyRadius^2
impulse         = pressureImpulse * projectedArea * falloff
deltaSpeed      = impulse / bodyMass
deltaVelocity   = radialDirection * deltaSpeed
```

Bodies whose surface distance exceeds the radius receive no response. A deterministic grid DDA checks the segment from the wall-offset explosion origin to the candidate center. A blocked candidate is retained in debug event data with zero applied impulse and its nonzero potential impulse.

This is a metric-consistent gameplay pressure-impulse model, not a fluid or fragmentation simulation.

## 4. Dynamic collision

The player and rocks are circles in X/Z. Dynamic collision uses:

```text
maximum body solver passes = 4
body restitution           = 0.10
body friction              = 0.35
rock-wall restitution      = 0.18
rock-wall friction         = 0.20
rock linear damping        = 1.5 /s
rock settle threshold      = 0.02 m/s
rock speed cap             = 20 m/s
```

The number of integration substeps is chosen from current maximum body speed and minimum body radius, bounded to eight. Circle/circle penetration correction and collision impulses are inverse-mass weighted.

Player motion has two velocity channels:

```text
locomotionVelocity = control-driven acceleration/braking
externalVelocity   = explosion and contact response
totalVelocity      = locomotionVelocity + externalVelocity
```

External player velocity damps at 2/s. Keeping it separate prevents the movement controller from erasing knockback on the next tick.

## 5. Authored scenarios

Scenario JSON v2 extends map v1:

```json
{
  "version": 2,
  "width": 24,
  "height": 24,
  "cells": [1, 1, 1],
  "playerSpawn": { "x": 3.5, "z": 3.5 },
  "entities": [
    { "kind": "rock", "archetype": "small", "x": 5, "z": 18.5 }
  ]
}
```

The abbreviated `cells` array above only illustrates shape; a real file contains exactly `width * height` entries.

- Map v1 remains accepted and produces an empty entity list.
- Save writes authored rock positions, not their displaced runtime positions.
- Reset and Restore positions reconstruct runtime rocks from authored state.
- Adding a wall or rock that overlaps authored or active bodies is rejected.
- Entering edit mode pauses the runtime. Leaving restores the run state that existed before editing.
- Rock pools are bounded at 64 active bodies and use dense typed arrays with swap-and-pop deletion.

## 6. Commands and probe surface

New tick-boundary actions:

```js
{ type: "loadScenario", json }
{ type: "placeRock", archetype: "small", x: 5, z: 6 }
{ type: "removeEntity", kind: "rock", id: 4 }
{ type: "restoreScenario" }
```

`loadMap` remains a compatibility alias for `loadScenario`.

New `window.__lantern` methods:

```js
saveScenario()
loadScenario(json)
listRockArchetypes()
canPlaceRock(archetype, x, z)
placeRock(archetype, x, z)
removeEntity("rock", id)
restoreScenario()
```

`saveMap()` and `loadMap()` remain compatibility aliases and now understand scenario v2.

Explosion events expose origin, radius, source projectile, owner, hit target, and per-body response records. Snapshots expose rock identity, archetype, mass, authored spawn identity, velocity, pool counters, and body/grid contacts.

## 7. Pipeline

```text
consume commands
prepare player locomotion
damp and substep player/rock dynamics
resolve grid and dynamic-body contacts
spawn fireball
sweep projectiles against grid and rocks
create explosion event
apply wall-occluded body impulses
emit visual particles
advance visual particles
publish snapshot and command record
```

The command log stores the initial scenario and rock pool configuration. Replay supports both new `initialScenario` recordings and legacy `initialMap` recordings.

## 8. Explicitly deferred

- health, damage, status effects, teams, and ownership rules beyond source identity
- destructible walls or rocks
- projectile momentum transfer separate from explosion impulse
- angular velocity, torque, rolling, and irregular rock shapes
- dynamic-body blast occlusion
- ~~particle collision with map walls~~ - addressed by the [0.2.0 particle-collision supplement](./0.2.0-particle-collision.md)
- entity-component-system migration
- fixed-point or cross-runtime lockstep guarantees

M0.1 keeps the force path independent from particles. M0.2 adds presentation-only map collision without changing gameplay physics.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.2.0-particle-collision.md">
# Project Lantern — 0.2.0: Map-Colliding Sparks

> **Version:** 0.2.0 (historical milestone M0.2)
>
> **Status:** implemented supplement to M0 and M0.1
> **Runtime:** browser JavaScript ES modules
> **Purpose:** make fireball sparks respect solid map cells without turning presentation particles into gameplay bodies.

The M0 simulation skeleton and M0.1 blast-force model remain authoritative for their original scopes. This document owns particle/map collision, its observability, and recording compatibility.

> **Historical lifecycle note:** M0.2.5 supersedes the lifetime, vertical-emission, radius, Ground-bounce default, and recording-schema details in this document. Wall collision remains owned by M0.2.

## 1. Player-visible behavior

- Fireball sparks collide with solid cells and map boundaries in X/Z.
- Sparks may ricochet repeatedly until their existing lifetime expires.
- The rendered particle radius may overlap a wall slightly because collision uses the particle center as a point.
- Walls are infinitely tall for particles. A spark's Y position never allows its center to cross a solid X/Z cell.
- Particles ignore the player, rocks, and other particles. They still cause no damage, force, or gameplay event.
- **Spark walls** controls runtime wall sweeps and defaults enabled.
- **Ground bounce** remains an independent vertical toggle with its M0 behavior.

The existing burst and ballistic ranges are unchanged:

```text
burst count              = 224
particle capacity        = 4,096
lifetime                 = 0.25-0.80 s
maximum horizontal speed = 7 m/s
gravity                  = -9.81 m/s2
initial Y                = 0.10 m
```

## 2. Emission and safe spawn placement

The seeded emitter first constructs the same bounded horizontal velocity as before. If that velocity points into the impacted surface, its normal component is mirrored into the outward half-plane. Mirroring preserves the sampled horizontal speed. Vertical velocity, lifetime, size, RNG sequencing, gravity, capacity, and burst count retain their existing ranges.

Every attempted particle spawn checks its X/Z point against the map, even when **Spark walls** is disabled. A reusable correction record moves an invalid point along the impact normal for at most eight solid-cell exits. If it still cannot reach a floor cell, the particle is rejected and the particle pool's cumulative `collisionDiscards` counter increments.

This bounded correction prevents bad corner or boundary contacts from creating particles inside walls without allowing an unbounded map search.

## 3. Swept point collision

`sweepPointAgainstGrid` is an allocation-free grid DDA. The caller provides one reusable output record:

```text
x, z       = earliest hit position
time       = normalized time in [0,1] along the requested sweep
cx, cz     = deterministic solid cell
nx, nz     = outward contact normal
```

The query visits crossed grid cells instead of sampling only the endpoint. Axial crossings return an axis normal. Exact corner crossings test both adjacent cells and the diagonal cell; closed or diagonal-only corners return a deterministic cell and a combined outward normal. Out-of-bounds cells use the map's existing solid-boundary rule.

Particle endpoints are rounded to the same `Float32` representation stored by the pool before the sweep. This prevents a mathematically safe double-precision endpoint from rounding into a solid cell after collision has already been checked.

## 4. Ricochet response

Each particle processes at most four wall contacts per fixed tick. After a hit it moves a small representable distance along the outward normal, reflects its horizontal velocity, and continues through the unused fraction of the timestep.

For incoming horizontal velocity `v` and outward unit normal `n`:

```text
normalSpeed = dot(v, n)
tangent     = v - normalSpeed * n
v'          = tangent * 0.95 - normalSpeed * 0.80 * n
```

Vertical velocity is not part of wall response. Gravity and the later ground check run independently. If the four-contact safety bound is reached, the particle remains at the last safe contact instead of consuming the remaining movement through a wall.

## 5. Pool state and observability

`ParticlePool` adds:

```text
wallBounceCount  Uint16Array, one value per active particle
wallBounces      cumulative reflected wall contacts
collisionDiscards cumulative failed spawn/runtime corrections
```

Swap-and-pop copies `wallBounceCount` with every other particle component and stable ID. Reset clears both cumulative counters.

Snapshots and inspector records expose `wallBounceCount`. Particle-pool telemetry exposes `wallBounces` and `collisionDiscards`. Particle wall impacts intentionally do not enter the 256-entry player/rock contact history; a full burst could otherwise erase more useful gameplay contact data every tick.

## 6. Schemas and replay

Snapshot, fixed-runtime metric, and command-recording schema is version 3. Scenario JSON remains version 2.

Schema-v3 recordings store the initial `particleWallCollision` mode in their configuration. Later changes continue to be normal tick-boundary `setDebugFlag` commands, so replay reconstructs the original mode sequence.

Schema-v2 recordings start replay with particle wall collision disabled. Those recordings predate map-colliding sparks, so this preserves their non-colliding runtime behavior.

## 7. Pipeline

```text
create explosion event and apply gameplay impulse
construct seeded outward particle velocity
sanitize particle spawn against map (always)
advance particle age and vertical velocity
if Spark walls:
  sanitize unexpected embedded point
  sweep point through grid
  reflect and consume unused time, up to four contacts
else:
  advance X/Z without a wall query
advance Y
apply unchanged Ground bounce or ground kill
publish particle state and pool counters
```

## 8. Verification and performance boundary

Automated coverage includes:

- axial, diagonal, closed-corner, and out-of-bounds sweeps
- high-speed one-cell-wall tunneling prevention
- outward-half-plane emission and exact reflection retention
- bounded spawn correction and counted rejection
- repeated corridor ricochet, floor-cell containment, and lifetime expiry
- no player or rock interaction
- independent wall and ground toggles
- swap-and-pop, snapshot, inspector, schema-v3 replay, and schema-v2 compatibility
- the existing 36,000-tick soak plus a 4,096-particle corridor stress case

The performance target remains simulation p99 below 8 ms with bounded pools and histories. Collision work is proportional to active particles and the small number of grid cells crossed during their fixed-tick movement.

## 9. Explicit non-goals

- particle/player, particle/rock, or particle/particle collision
- damage, impulse, pressure, ownership, teams, or gameplay contacts from particles
- particle radius collision or rendered-shape-perfect contact
- finite wall height or particle traversal over walls
- ~~lifetime extension~~ - addressed by the [0.2.5 particle-lifecycle supplement](./0.2.5-particle-lifecycle.md)
- an unbounded or configurable bounce-count rule
- GPU particle physics or a general physics-engine integration
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.2.5-particle-lifecycle.md">
# Project Lantern — 0.2.5: Size-Linked Ember Lifecycles

> **Version:** 0.2.5 (historical milestone M0.2.5)
>
> **Status:** implemented supplement to M0.2
> **Runtime:** browser JavaScript ES modules
> **Purpose:** connect visible spark size, lifetime, shrink, and ground response while retaining bounded presentation-only particles.

M0.2 remains authoritative for map collision. This document owns particle emission height, size-linked lifetime, age-based visible radius, the single ground bounce, and schema-3 lifecycle compatibility.

## 1. Player-visible behavior

- Maximum radius stays uniformly randomized from `0.025-0.085m`.
- Larger sparks generally live longer; nearby sizes retain slight seeded lifetime variation.
- Spark and ground-shadow radius shrink smoothly toward zero over normalized age.
- Emission preserves some high sparks but biases the shower toward low arcs and ground contact.
- Ground bounce defaults enabled and remains independent from **Spark walls**.
- Each particle may bounce from `Y=0` once. Its next ground contact settles it.
- Bounce never extends lifetime, and particle collision remains point-based in X/Z.

Burst count stays 224 and capacity stays 4,096.

## 2. Balanced M0.2.5 profile

For sampled maximum size `size`:

```text
s        = (size - 0.025) / (0.085 - 0.025)
lifetime = clamp(0.22 + 0.83*s + (lifetimeRoll - 0.5)*0.12, 0.18, 1.10)
vy       = 0.6 + 5.9*verticalRoll^2
```

The six seeded emission draws retain their existing order: horizontal angle, horizontal speed, outward bias, vertical roll, lifetime roll, and size roll. This keeps RNG advancement aligned between supported profiles while allowing their derived particle values to differ.

The squared vertical roll retains occasional `6.5m/s` launches while concentrating more particles near the `0.6m/s` minimum. A full seeded burst should place 50-70% of particles on the ground before lifetime expiry.

## 3. Radius over lifetime

The pool's existing `size` component remains maximum radius. Current visible radius is derived without another hot-loop array:

```text
remainingLife = 1 - clamp(age / lifetime, 0, 1)
currentSize   = size * remainingLife^0.65
```

Snapshots expose both `size` and `currentSize`. The renderer uses `currentSize` for the spark and its shadow. Hover/picking uses the current radius plus the existing selection tolerance. Inspector `radius` is current size and `maxRadius` is the sampled maximum.

Radius does not affect wall collision, ground contact, damage, or force.

## 4. Ground response

On the first `Y<=0` contact while **Ground bounce** is enabled:

```text
y  = 0
vy = abs(vy) * 0.45
vx = vx * 0.82
vz = vz * 0.82
```

The per-particle `bounced` flag prevents another rebound. On later ground contacts, the balanced profile clamps `y` and `vy` to zero and retains 82% of horizontal speed per fixed tick, creating a short slide. The ember remains active and continues shrinking until its assigned age limit removes it. Disabling **Ground bounce** still removes it on the first contact.

The legacy `m0.2` profile retains its historical second-contact removal so schema-v2 and schema-v3 recordings replay exactly.

`ParticlePool.groundBounces` is a cumulative counter exposed with wall bounces and collision discards in snapshot/UI telemetry. Pool reset clears it.

## 5. Profiles and replay

Snapshot, fixed-runtime metric, and command-recording schema is version 4. Scenario JSON remains version 2.

Two frozen profiles exist:

```text
m0.2.5-balanced = size-linked lifetime, shrinking radius, low-biased arcs,
                  45% vertical / 82% horizontal ground response
m0.2           = independent 0.25-0.80s lifetime, constant radius,
                  2.2-7.5m/s vertical launch,
                  35% vertical / 75% horizontal ground response
```

Schema-v4 recordings capture `particleProfile`, initial `particleBounce`, and initial `particleWallCollision`. Later toggle changes remain tick-boundary commands.

Schema-v3 recordings select `m0.2`, assume the historical initial Ground-bounce default `false`, and retain their stored wall-collision mode. Schema-v2 also selects `m0.2` with wall collision disabled. Unknown profile names fail instead of silently selecting another behavior.

## 6. Verification and boundaries

Automated coverage verifies:

- exact maximum-size and lifetime bounds plus separated small/large lifetime quartiles
- approximately 50-70% ground contact for a 4,096-particle seeded burst
- current radius at spawn, half-life, and near expiry
- snapshot, picking, and inspector use of current/max radius
- exact 45%/82% response, one-bounce settling, age-authoritative expiry, and toggle-off kill
- cumulative ground telemetry and reset behavior
- bit-for-bit M0.2 seeded particle and ground-response fixtures
- schema-v4 replay plus schema-v3 and schema-v2 compatibility
- existing collision, 36,000-tick soak, and 4,096-particle corridor performance checks

Particles remain presentation-only: no player, rock, particle, damage, force, or finite-height wall interaction is added.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.5.0-spell-lab.md">
# Lantern 0.5.0 Spell Lab and Versioned Fireball Authoring

## Contract

Lantern 0.5.0 adds one reusable spell-authoring seam and one spell handler: Fireball. Spell Lab is a collapsible, non-modal overlay on desktop and a bottom drawer at `620px` and narrower. The rest of the arena remains interactive while the panel is open.

The registry owns stable spell code `1` for `fireball`, a player cooldown per spell code, an immutable revision map, and a monotonically increasing revision counter. A complete validated Apply creates the next revision. A cast captures spell code, definition revision, effect ID, and 32-bit effect seed. Every later authoritative or presentation decision for that effect resolves through those captured values.

The product intent is a developer tuning catalog first. Revisions are internal runtime identity that makes live tuning and replay safe; they are not a player-facing spell-history feature. Limited cosmetic customization could later reuse validated palette or presentation fields, but this release defines no player persistence, ownership, trading, loadout, or networking contract for customized spells.

This release does not add mana, loadouts, damage, sounds, more spells, pool-capacity authoring, fixed-tick authoring, collision-iteration authoring, or GPU-topology controls.

## Fireball definition format v1

Documents are complete JSON objects. Partial patches, unknown fields, non-finite numbers, malformed colors, and out-of-range values are rejected atomically. The descriptor table in `src/spells/fireball_definition.js` is the single source for defaults, labels, tooltips, units, steps, and scalar validation.

```json
{
  "formatVersion": 1,
  "cast": {
    "cooldown": 0.2
  },
  "projectile": {
    "speed": 9,
    "radius": 0.12,
    "lifetime": 4,
    "spawnGap": 0.02
  },
  "impact": {
    "blastRadius": 2.5,
    "pressureImpulse": 800,
    "visualLifetime": 0.2
  },
  "emission": {
    "burstCount": 224,
    "spawnHeight": 0.1,
    "gravity": -9.81,
    "horizontalSpeedMinimum": 1.4,
    "horizontalSpeedMaximum": 5.8,
    "horizontalSpeedCap": 7,
    "outwardBiasMinimum": 0.2,
    "outwardBiasMaximum": 1.1,
    "verticalMinimum": 0.6,
    "verticalRange": 5.9,
    "verticalPower": 2
  },
  "particleLifecycle": {
    "sizeMinimum": 0.025,
    "sizeMaximum": 0.085,
    "lifetimeMinimum": 0.18,
    "lifetimeMaximum": 1.1,
    "lifetimeBase": 0.22,
    "lifetimeSizeScale": 0.83,
    "lifetimeJitter": 0.12,
    "shrinkExponent": 0.65
  },
  "collision": {
    "groundMode": "bounce-settle",
    "groundVerticalRetention": 0.45,
    "groundHorizontalRetention": 0.82,
    "wallCollision": true,
    "wallNormalRetention": 0.8,
    "wallTangentialRetention": 0.95
  },
  "palette": {
    "projectile": "#FF834D",
    "core": "#FFE4A3",
    "hot": "#FFBD59",
    "ember": "#FF7814",
    "decay": "#F01F06",
    "gradientSplit": 0.58,
    "perCastHueVariation": 3,
    "perCastSaturationVariation": 0.02,
    "perCastBrightnessVariation": 0,
    "perParticleHueVariation": 0,
    "perParticleSaturationVariation": 0,
    "perParticleBrightnessVariation": 0
  },
  "presentation": {
    "projectileEmissiveStrength": 3.8,
    "particleEmissiveStrength": 2.6,
    "flightLightIntensity": 22,
    "flightLightRange": 3,
    "flightLightDecay": 2,
    "impactLightIntensity": 52,
    "impactLightRange": 5,
    "impactLightDecay": 2,
    "sparkLightIntensity": 13,
    "sparkLightRange": 1.5,
    "sparkLightDecay": 2
  }
}
```

The built-in values retain the 0.4.0 Fireball mechanics and energy targets. Newly exposed per-particle hue, saturation, and brightness jitter default to zero.

### Hard authoring limits

| Group | Limits |
| --- | --- |
| Cast | cooldown `0-5s` |
| Projectile | radius `0.02-1m`; speed `0.1-40m/s`; lifetime `0.1-20s`; spawn gap `0-1m` |
| Impact | blast radius `0-12m`; pressure impulse `0-5000`; visual life `0.02-2s` |
| Emission | integer burst `0-1024`; spawn height `0-2m`; gravity `-40-10m/s²`; horizontal speed, cap, bias, vertical minimum, and vertical range `0-30m/s`; vertical power `0-4` |
| Lifecycle | size `0.005-0.5m`; lifetime values `0.02-10s`; size scale `-10-10s`; shrink exponent `0-4` |
| Collision | retention `0-1`; ground mode `bounce-settle` or `remove`; wall collision boolean |
| Palette | exact `#RRGGBB`; gradient split `0-1`; hue variation `0-30°`; saturation/brightness variation `0-0.25` |
| Presentation | emissive `0-12`; light intensity `0-150`; range `0-20m`; decay `0-4` |

Validation additionally requires horizontal-speed minimum ≤ maximum, outward-bias minimum ≤ maximum, size minimum ≤ maximum, and lifetime minimum ≤ maximum. Values are rejected, never silently clamped.

## Distribution and lifecycle formulas

Each particle ordinal derives a sample seed from the effect seed. Named hash lanes make the dimensions independent:

```text
angle             = angle-lane × 2π
horizontal speed  = mix(speed minimum, speed maximum, speed-lane)
outward bias      = mix(bias minimum, bias maximum, bias-lane)
vertical velocity = vertical minimum + vertical range × vertical-lane ^ vertical power
size              = mix(size minimum, size maximum, size-lane)
normalized size   = (size - size minimum) / (size maximum - size minimum)
lifetime          = clamp(
  lifetime base
  + lifetime size scale × normalized size
  + (lifetime-lane - 0.5) × lifetime jitter,
  lifetime minimum,
  lifetime maximum
)
visible radius    = maximum size × remaining normalized life ^ shrink exponent
```

Angle, speed, bias, vertical velocity, lifetime, size, hue, saturation, and brightness are separate semantic lanes. Increasing the burst count cannot alter existing ordinals or the seed of a later successful cast. Palette-only variation cannot consume simulation RNG.

## Draft and cast workflow

- Numeric rows pair a slider and exact numeric input. Color rows pair a swatch and `#RRGGBB` field. Units and descriptor text are visible as labels/tooltips.
- Essentials opens initially. Projectile, Distribution, Lifetime, Collision, Palette, Emissive, and Lighting are independently collapsible.
- **Apply** validates a complete document against the displayed revision and creates an immutable revision for future casts.
- **Revert to applied** replaces the draft with the current applied document.
- **Reset draft to defaults** changes only the draft. It never applies automatically.
- **Copy JSON** and **Download JSON** operate only on a valid draft.
- **Import JSON** replaces the draft after parsing. Valid or invalid imported content remains unapplied until an explicit successful Apply.
- Simulation reset removes active effects and resets the successful-cast sequence while preserving applied revisions and the panel draft. The current applied documents become the new recording baseline.
- Browser reload returns to built-in defaults. Spell Lab does not use URL or local-storage persistence.

Normal LMB casts and **Recast last target** share one cast-command builder. Recast remains disabled until a target exists and while the normal Fireball cooldown is active. **Lock seed** makes both paths write the visible hexadecimal seed into the cast command. **New variation** creates and locks a new uint32 preview seed. Without a lock, a successful cast derives:

```text
hash32(simulation seed, spell code, successful-cast sequence)
```

Rejected casts do not advance the sequence. Successful automatic seeds are written explicitly into schema-v5 command history.

**Clear active effects** removes active Fireball projectiles, particles, retained visual impact events, and therefore their light leases. It does not reverse impulses already applied to the player or rocks.

## Authority boundary

Fixed-tick simulation owns projectile speed, radius, lifetime, and collision; blast radius and pressure impulse; emission distribution; particle gravity, lifecycle, ground response, and map/boundary collision. Those values always come from the revision captured by the cast.

Palette colors, emissive strengths, bloom response, point-light intensity/range/decay, mesh height, and shadows are presentation only. They cannot change collision, force, visibility, AI, command ordering, or replay truth. Debug Layers **Ground bounce** and **Spark walls** remain global test overrides around the captured per-spell collision behavior.

Canvas2D, Three.js instances, impact lights, and spark-carrier lights call the same allocation-free palette sampler. The existing `lightColorVariation` URL/probe key remains compatible and is labeled **Spell color variation**; it gates all geometry, particle, and light variation.

The Three.js path preallocates projectile and particle instance color/emissive attributes at full pool capacity before `compileAsync` warmup. Applying definitions writes resident attribute bytes only. Geometry, materials, TSL nodes, buffers, pipelines, and the resident eight-slot effect groups are not rebuilt. TrueSight continues masking meshes, emissive bloom, lights, and shadows before presentation.

## Commands, snapshots, and probes

The existing cast shape remains valid:

```js
{ cast: { x, z } }
{ cast: { x, z, spellId: "fireball", variationSeed: 0x12345678 } }
```

Omitted `spellId` means Fireball. New fixed-tick actions are:

```js
{ type: "applySpellDefinition", spellId, expectedRevision, definition }
{ type: "clearSpellEffects", spellId }
```

Snapshots use schema v5. Their compact `spells` table contains the current revision and only definitions referenced by live pools or retained events. Projectiles, particles, and events reference it with `spellCode` and `definitionRevision`; they also carry `effectId` and `effectSeed`. Particles add `sampleOrdinal` and `sampleSeed`. Swap removal copies every identity field.

The browser probe exposes:

```js
window.__lantern.listSpells()
window.__lantern.getSpellDefinition("fireball")
window.__lantern.applySpellDefinition("fireball", definition, expectedRevision)
window.__lantern.castSpell("fireball", x, z, { variationSeed })
window.__lantern.clearSpellEffects("fireball")
window.__lantern.spellDiagnostics("fireball")
```

Validation and malformed probe arguments return `{ ok: false, errors: [...] }` with paths and error codes. Diagnostics include current/applied revision, retained revisions, next automatic seed, cooldown, active counts, pool drops, latest impact, and sampled speed/lifetime/size/color ranges. Entity inspector records include spell, revision, effect ID, and seed.

## Recording compatibility

Schema-v5 recording configuration stores initial spell definitions and revision counters. Apply commands store deep-cloned complete documents. Replay restores the registry baseline, applies actions before casting in each tick, and reconstructs explicit seeds and effect-local samples exactly.

Schema-v2, v3, and v4 recordings use frozen legacy Fireball mechanics and global RNG consumption. They do not pass through the versioned handler. Schema v4 preserves its recorded particle profile and initial collision switches; schema v3 uses exact `m0.2`; schema v2 additionally disables spark-wall collision.

## Extension seam

A later spell adds one stable numeric registry code, one strict definition validator/descriptor set, and one named handler. Casting dispatches by handler after registry lookup; pools and events already carry generic spell/revision/effect identity. New handlers must preserve fixed-tick authority, explicit revision capture, bounded history, independent semantic randomness, structured validation, and presentation-only rendering fields.

## Validation

Automated acceptance covers strict validation and JSON round trips; future-casts-only revisions; impact, particle, collision, palette, and light capture; pool-swap identity; revision pruning; reset baselines; clear semantics; same-tick Apply-plus-cast ordering; pinned random fixtures and lane independence; schema-v5 exact replay plus schema-v2/v3/v4 fixtures; stable Three.js materials/nodes/attributes/lights; and maximum-definition/pool-saturation stress under the `8ms` simulation p99 target.

Run:

```bash
npm run check
npm run test:soak
npm run test:soak:sight
npm run test:soak:spell
git diff --check
```

Perceptual acceptance still requires Canvas2D, automatic WebGPU/3D, forced WebGL2, and LAN-phone WebGL2 checks. Compare locked seeds while running and paused, exercise import/export and maximum-safe tuning, and inspect TrueSight plus bloom/shadow combinations for concealed color, light, particle, shadow, emissive, or GPU-validation leakage. Low and Balanced before/after performance captures remain a human release gate.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.6.0-obelisk-combat.md">
# Lantern 0.6.0 Obelisk Combat Foundation

## Contract

Lantern 0.6.0 adds one replay-safe combat encounter to the default debug arena without adding stages or changing the Fireball authoring format. A scenario-authored, invulnerable obelisk stands at world position `(20.5, 18.5)` on solid cell `(20, 18)`. It behaves exactly like a wall for actor motion, projectiles, blast line-of-effect, AI line-of-sight, and TrueSight, but both renderers replace that wall cell with procedural obelisk geometry.

Legacy scenario v2 and map v1 documents remain valid sandbox arenas. Because they contain no obelisk, they spawn no enemies. Scenario v3 permits existing rocks plus at most one `{ "kind": "obelisk", "x", "z" }` entity. The obelisk must be cell-centered, occupy an in-bounds solid cell, and avoid player or rock overlap. Its entity cannot be removed and its supporting cell cannot be erased in the editor.

This release explicitly adds no stage counter, end portal, obelisk damage or scaling, loot, mana, loadouts, sound, contact damage, player customization, enemy-specific spell definitions, networking, or multiplayer authority.

## Encounter and basic wizard

The encounter attempts its first spawn on fixed tick 1 and another every 1,800 ticks, or 30 seconds. It holds at most four living enemies. Capped or obstructed attempts are counted and skipped without retries or queued debt. Every attempt advances through the eight adjacent cell centers in fixed order: north, east, south, west, northeast, southeast, southwest, northwest.

Each enemy has a stable pool ID and stable spawn sequence. The four-entry typed-array pool uses swap removal, copying identity, position history, locomotion/external velocity channels, physical values, health/regeneration state, cooldown, cast sequence, ready tick, line-of-sight, and AI state together.

`basic-wizard-v1` always knows the player's current simulation position. It approaches beyond `9m`, withdraws inside `6m`, and holds between `6m` and `9m`. It uses the same acceleration, braking, wall collision, wall sliding, and dynamic-body collision model as the player. It attempts a direct shot every 75 ticks (1.25 seconds) and also observes the currently applied Fireball cooldown. A blocked shot remains ready until line-of-sight and cooldown both permit it.

The basic profile intentionally does not strafe, lead targets, dodge, pathfind, or retreat to regenerate. Rocks and actors are handled by local collision response only, so walls can trap or delay a wizard. TrueSight does not contribute knowledge to this behavior.

## Shared spell and combat rules

Player and enemy wizards cast through the same global Fireball registry. Apply actions execute before casts in a tick and affect future casts from either team immediately. Every projectile, impact, particle, palette sample, and light lease retains its captured definition revision after Apply or caster death. Enemy casts do not advance the player's cooldown, successful-cast sequence, or Spell Lab preview seed.

Player seed derivation remains unchanged. An enemy effect seed is derived from simulation seed, stable enemy spawn sequence, spell code, and that enemy's successful-cast sequence. Both paths are independent of global simulation RNG and one another.

Player and enemy wizards share a `0.3m` radius, `75kg` mass, player movement values, `100` maximum health, and `1 HP/s` regeneration after 300 damage-free ticks. Health damage is a symmetric non-authorable combat rule:

```text
direct opponent hit = 25
splash damage = 25 × clamp(1 - surfaceDistance / capturedBlastRadius, 0, 1)
```

Solid cells, including the obelisk, block splash damage. Casters and same-team actors are immune to health damage and ignored by same-team projectile collision. The existing physical explosion impulse remains neutral and can still move casters, allies, opponents, and rocks. Projectiles fired by a dead enemy stay active; dead enemies compact only after all projectile processing for that tick.

## Defeat and reset

Zero player health enters `defeated` for 90 fixed ticks (1.5 seconds). Autonomous movement, casting, enemies, projectiles, particles, regeneration, and encounter cadence freeze. Movement and cast commands are ignored, while explicit reset, Apply, and Clear actions remain accepted. The UI reports `DEFEATED`, the remaining restart time, and a non-modal overlay so Spell Lab remains accessible.

When the countdown expires, reset uses the same simulation seed and restores full health, authored rocks, encounter cadence, empty enemy/effect pools, deterministic IDs/sequences, and an empty command-log baseline. It preserves the currently applied Fireball revision; the browser-owned Spell Lab draft also remains intact.

## Snapshots, replay, and diagnostics

Application/package/UI version is `0.6.0`. Snapshot and command-recording schema is v6; scenario schema is v3. Map v1, Fireball definition v1, and performance-report v2 do not change.

Schema-v6 snapshots add gameplay and AI profiles, level/defeat state, encounter timing and skips, obelisks, enemies, player/enemy health and regeneration, projectile owner kind/team, structured player/enemy explosion ownership and hits, bounded combat events, and enemy-pool telemetry. `queryAt`, pinning, and inspector records recognize `enemyWizard` and `obelisk`.

Schema-v6 recording configuration stores:

```json
{
  "gameplayProfile": "obelisk-duel-v1",
  "enemyAiProfile": "basic-wizard-v1"
}
```

Enemy decisions remain deterministic autonomous simulation and are not written as input commands. Schema-v5 recordings keep versioned Fireball behavior but select frozen pre-combat gameplay. Schema-v2 through v4 continue through their genuine historical profiles. The browser probe `window.__lantern.encounterDiagnostics()` returns level state, restart timing, spawn cadence/skips, enemy identities, health, cooldowns, cast sequences, and recent combat events.

## Presentation

Canvas2D and Three.js render the same hostile silhouette in a distinct color and the same procedural obelisk without external art or new lights. Every living actor has a screen-right `0.10m × 0.90m` vertical health track filled from the bottom: green above 50%, amber from 25% through 50%, and red at or below 25%.

The Three.js adapter creates all four enemy instances and five health-track/fill instances before asynchronous renderer warmup. Their geometries, materials, instance buffers, and identities remain resident. Fireball point-light capacity and lease topology are unchanged.

TrueSight conceals each hidden enemy mesh and health bar. It also conceals every hostile projectile, particle, emissive response, and light through the existing shared effect masking path. Concealment is presentation-only and cannot alter collision, casting, damage, AI, recordings, or replay.

## Validation

Automated acceptance covers scenario-v3 validation and legacy imports; protected cells; deterministic spawn cadence, cap, skips, and reset; actor/grid/rock collision; pool-swap identity; independent caster cooldowns, sequences, and seeds; same-tick Apply ordering; captured revisions after death; direct/splash damage, falloff, occlusion, immunity, pass-through, neutral impulse, regeneration, enemy death, and defeat timing; exact schema-v6 replay and genuine schema-v2/v3/v4/v5 fixtures; Canvas2D/Three.js health and obelisk parity; TrueSight-ready resident materials; bounded event history; and maximum-definition four-enemy combat soak under the `8ms` simulation p99 target.

Run:

```bash
npm run check
npm run test:soak
npm run test:soak:sight
npm run test:soak:spell
npm run test:soak:combat
git diff --check
```

Human review remains the acceptance gate for Canvas2D, automatic Three.js, forced WebGL 2, and a trusted-LAN phone route. That visual matrix should confirm obelisk readability, enemy distinction, screen-right health placement/fill, defeat overlay timing, TrueSight concealment, and the absence of new warmup or lighting-topology regressions.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.7.0-tactical-wizard-ai.md">
# Lantern 0.7.0 Tactical Wizard AI

## Contract

Lantern 0.7.0 replaces the live `basic-wizard-v1` encounter behavior with `tactical-wizard-v1`. It advances application/package/UI and snapshot/recording schema to v7 while leaving scenario v3, map v1, Fireball definition v1, performance-report v2, combat damage, encounter cadence, actor physics, and presentation topology unchanged.

The four-entry enemy typed-array pool remains the authority for stable identity, health, velocity channels, cooldowns, successful-cast sequences, and AI state. Its swap removal now also moves navigation, movement-goal, strafe, aim, threat, dodge, and retreat components as one identity.

## Shared navigation field

All enemies consume one simulation-authoritative distance field rooted at the player's cell. Dijkstra expansion uses integer cost `10` for cardinal neighbors and `14` for diagonals. Neighbor order is north, east, south, west, northeast, southeast, southwest, northwest. A diagonal is traversable only when its destination and both adjoining cardinal cells are floor, preventing corner cutting.

The completed and building cost arrays, binary-heap queue, queue positions, and tie sequences are preallocated for the current map dimensions. At most 2,048 cells are expanded in one fixed tick. A rebuild starts when the player changes cells or an accepted tile/load command changes the map revision. The previous completed array remains readable while the replacement is building. If no field has completed, movement falls back to the 0.6 direct range-correction behavior; an unreachable or locally exhausted gradient also uses that bounded direct fallback.

Approach follows strictly decreasing finite cost. Range withdrawal and low-health retreat follow strictly increasing finite cost. Equal gradient choices retain the fixed neighbor order. Rocks and actors are intentionally absent from the field and continue through existing local dynamic collision, so they may deflect or temporarily block an otherwise valid route.

## Engagement and deterministic strafe

The `6-9m` engagement band remains authoritative. Beyond `9m` the wizard approaches; inside `6m` it withdraws; inside the band it moves tangentially at `3.5m/s`. Initial left/right direction and each `90-180` tick interval come from named hashes of simulation seed, stable enemy spawn sequence, and enemy-local decision sequence. The side flips at every scheduled change.

These decisions never consume global simulation RNG. Adding another enemy, changing particle count or palette variation, or making unrelated player casts cannot shift an existing enemy's strafe schedule.

## Intercept aim and shared spell updates

Every tactical aim update solves the smallest positive constant-velocity interception time from current authoritative player position/velocity and the currently applied Fireball projectile speed. No positive solution falls back to direct aim. A valid solution is clamped to `min(projectile lifetime, 1.5s)`, then 75% of that lead is applied to the target point.

Current-player grid line-of-sight remains the cast gate. A blocked or cooling shot stays ready. Apply actions still precede autonomous casts in a tick, so a newly applied speed affects that tick's enemy intercept immediately. Enemy cooldowns, cast sequences, and effect seeds remain caster-local, and existing effects retain captured revisions.

## Hostile-projectile dodge

A player-team Fireball is a threat only when all of these are true:

- closest approach is from `0.25s` through `0.90s` ahead and within remaining projectile lifetime;
- current center distance is at least `2m`;
- predicted miss distance is no greater than enemy radius plus projectile radius plus `0.20m`.

Simultaneous threats choose the earliest approach, then the smaller miss, effect ID, and projectile ID. The wizard evaluates both directions perpendicular to projectile travel across the full 18-tick map-clear dodge path. Among legal sides it chooses the greater predicted closest-approach clearance; an exact tie uses the enemy-local `dodge-tie` hash lane.

Dodge locomotion is immediately `6m/s` for 18 fixed ticks. It may temporarily override approach, strafe, withdrawal, or retreat movement without changing retreat status. When the dodge ends, 105 complete cooldown ticks must pass before a new dodge can begin.

## Retreat and regeneration

At `30` health or below, the wizard enters retreat, stops autonomous casting, and follows increasing navigation cost. Normal damage resets the existing five-second regeneration delay; regeneration remains `1 HP/s`. Dodge can interrupt the retreat vector but cannot clear the retreat flag. The wizard re-engages only when health is at least `60` at a decision tick.

## Schema, replay, and diagnostics

Schema-v7 recording configuration stores:

```json
{
  "gameplayProfile": "obelisk-duel-v1",
  "enemyAiProfile": "tactical-wizard-v1"
}
```

Schema-v6 recordings accept only the frozen obelisk/basic pairing (or their historical pre-combat pairing) and reproduce direct aim without tactical state affecting behavior. Schema-v2 through v5 retain their earlier frozen boundaries. Tactical decisions remain autonomous simulation rather than synthetic commands.

Snapshots and inspector records expose global field build/version/revision state plus each enemy's behavior state, movement goal, field cost/version, strafe side/change timer, predicted aim and lead time, tracked threat effect/projectile, dodge timers/direction, and retreat flag. `window.__lantern.enemyDiagnostics(id?)` returns the same read-only state for every enemy or one stable ID. No AI editor, mutation command, or Spell Lab AI field is added.

## Presentation and validation

Canvas2D and Three.js consume the extended snapshot without new meshes, materials, buffers, lights, art, or warmup topology. TrueSight concealment remains presentation-only and cannot perturb navigation, aim, dodge, retreat, hashes, or replay.

Automated acceptance covers corridor/doorway reachability, disconnected regions, diagonal corner prevention, fixed ties, bounded incremental work, stale-field use, map invalidation, intercept cases and clamping, immediate spell-speed updates, LOS gating, genuine and rejected threats, legal dodge sides/cooldown/simultaneous selection, strafe schedules/range correction, retreat/casting suppression/recovery, pool compaction, deterministic independence, exact schema-v7 replay, frozen schema-v6 direct aim, and all legacy replay fixtures.

Run:

```bash
npm run check
npm run test:soak
npm run test:soak:sight
npm run test:soak:spell
npm run test:soak:combat
npm run test:soak:ai
git diff --check
```

Human acceptance remains the Canvas2D, automatic Three.js, forced WebGL 2, and trusted-LAN phone matrix. Rendering should remain visually identical to 0.6.1 apart from diagnostic values and the enemies' new movement/casting behavior.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.8.0-visual-perception-hunting.md">
# Lantern 0.8.0 Visual Perception and Hunting

## Contract and release boundary

Lantern 0.8.0 replaces the live omniscient tactical enemy with
`perceptive-wizard-v1` and advances application/package/UI plus snapshot and
command-recording schema to v8. Scenario v3, map v1, Fireball definition v1,
performance-report v2, combat rules, and the default four-alive encounter
cadence remain unchanged.

Replay behavior is selected by the recording schema and stays frozen:

- schema v8 records `enemyCapacity: 64`, `encounterMaximumAlive: 4`, and the
  perceptive wizard profile;
- schema v7 reconstructs the historical four-entry enemy pool and exact
  omniscient `tactical-wizard-v1` behavior;
- schema v6 reconstructs the historical four-entry pool and
  `basic-wizard-v1` behavior;
- schema v2 through v5 remain pre-combat.

Enemy decisions remain autonomous fixed-step simulation, not synthetic input
commands. This release adds no sound, squad knowledge, networking, co-op actor,
friendly/critter perception, or lighting-driven detection.

## Geometry-only perception and facing

Every live v8 enemy owns an independent perception state:

`unaware → noticing → engaged → hunting → returning → unaware`

Dodge and low-health retreat are movement overlays and never erase this state
or its personal memory. A visual check succeeds only when the target center is
within `12m`, grid line of sight through walls and the obelisk is clear, and the
target is either inside the `120°` cone around authoritative facing or within
the `1.5m` 360-degree close-awareness radius. Rocks, lights, darkness,
TrueSight, particles, presentation height, and renderer state do not participate.

Checks run at `12Hz`, staggered over five deterministic lanes chosen from stable
enemy spawn sequence. First contact enters `noticing`; 15 uninterrupted fixed
ticks of qualifying samples confirm the player. Any failed noticing sample
resets the buildup. Hunting or returning enemies with existing visual memory
reacquire on their first successful sample. A mob with no confirmed target,
including one responding only to damage, must complete the normal buildup.

Facing is stored normalized and turns by at most `180°/s`. A visible target has
first priority, movement direction second, and state-specific scanning third.
Each guard stores its spawn position. Default-arena guards initially face away
from the obelisk; other scenarios choose one of eight deterministic headings.
Unaware guards sweep `±45°` around that heading on a six-second triangle wave
with an enemy-local phase. A guard displaced more than `0.5m` returns to post.

## Personal memory, search, and guard return

Every successful visual sample updates only that enemy's last-seen target
position, velocity, and tick. Losing sight immediately suppresses casting and
sends the enemy toward its personal last-seen point. Search begins within
`0.75m` of that point or after a 12-second travel timeout.

Search lasts exactly eight seconds. Candidate cells use reachable radii 1, 2,
and 3 in north, east, south, west, northeast, southeast, southwest, northwest
order, with a deterministic enemy-local rotation and reversal. The mob chooses
another candidate on arrival or after 90 ticks; when none is reachable it scans
in place. Search expiry starts guard return. Arrival clears perception memory
and returns the enemy to unaware. A map edit can disconnect a guard, but the
current reachable cell becomes the new guard only after the destination cache
has continuously proven the old guard unreachable for 12 seconds.

Unseen direct or splash damage starts or restarts an eight-second search around
the damage or impact point. The clue contains neither attacker identity nor the
player's hidden position, never shares between enemies, and cannot replace
existing visual memory.

## Tactical and combat interaction

Engaged enemies retain the 0.7.0 `6-9m` range control, deterministic strafe,
intercept aim, dodge, and health retreat. Casting requires `engaged`, no active
retreat, and a fresh same-tick cone/range/grid-line-of-sight check. A blocked
shot remains ready. Current authoritative player position and velocity are
used for aim only after that check; remembered coordinates are never fired on.
A same-tick Spell Lab Apply still precedes the autonomous cast, so the newly
applied projectile speed participates in intercept calculation immediately.

A hostile projectile is dodge-eligible only when it passes the frozen threat
calculation and is itself visible under the same cone, close-awareness, and
grid-occlusion rules. Seeing it reveals neither owner nor player and contributes
nothing to player exposure. Movement precedence is dodge, retreat, then
perception/tactical intent. Retreat uses the visible target field or latest
known hostile point while perception and search timers continue, and normal
behavior resumes at 60 health.

## Bounded data and 50-mob scaling

The live enemy typed-array pool has 64 entries while the authored encounter
still caps at four alive. Perception state/source, generic target identity,
samples, exposure, facing, guard, memory, hunt/search, stimulus, and navigation
identity are initialized, reset, and swap-moved with each stable actor. The
projectile pool increases from 128 to 256; particles remain 4,096 with bounded
drops for excessive Spell Lab effects. Legacy v6/v7 replay constructs its
historical four-enemy/128-projectile capacities.

The v7 player-rooted field remains frozen for legacy replay. Live v8 movement
uses a bounded destination cache with four pinned future actor-target slots and
64 shared goal-cell slots. One completed cost array belongs to each slot, while
all rebuilds share one preallocated Dijkstra builder and heap. Requests for the
same goal share a reference; referenced goals cannot be evicted. Stable slot
order and a global 2,048-expansion budget bound work each tick. Previous
completed data remains diagnostic while a target cell or map revision rebuilds,
but movement falls back to direct Plan-One wall sliding until the requested
field is current. Target approach descends target cost, withdrawal ascends
hostile cost, and hunt/return descends destination cost.

A preallocated map-cell broadphase replaces quadratic actor, rock, swept
projectile-hit, and dodge-candidate scans. Fixed typed candidate storage
deduplicates entries, then sorts them into the historical body-class and pool
index order before collision, contact, impulse, or first-hit resolution. Rocks
and actors remain local dynamic obstacles. Brute-force mode remains a test
oracle, not a second gameplay solver.

All scan, search, facing, strafe, and dodge decisions use named enemy-local hash
lanes. Global RNG, another enemy's cast sequence, particles, palette variation,
lighting, and TrueSight cannot perturb an existing mob's choices.

## Snapshots, diagnostics, and presentation

Each enemy snapshot and `window.__lantern.enemyDiagnostics(id?)` expose the
perception state/source, current visibility/sample/exposure, facing,
candidate/confirmed generic target identity, guard/base heading, personal
last-seen data, hunt anchor/search/timers/sequence, damage stimulus, and
destination-field slot/key/cost/version/stale/building state.

Global diagnostics report the bounded destination slots, active builder,
references, expansion budget, and map invalidation. A 128-entry event ring
retains detection, loss, search, return, damage-alert, reacquisition, and
awareness-clear events; the latest 32 plus retained/capacity/dropped telemetry
are copied to diagnostics.

AI View keeps its read-only Off/Selected/All semantics. Selected and All draw
the perception cone and close radius, facing/exposure, targets, memory or impact
clue, search goal, guard, and navigation state. Labels distinguish TrueSight's
**player sight** from authoritative **mob vision**. Ordinary Canvas2D and
Three.js hostile silhouettes share one procedural facing-marker footprint. It
shares hostile TrueSight concealment and adds no light; perception cones never
appear outside AI View. Before Three.js warmup the renderer preallocates 64
enemy instances, 64 markers, and 65 health tracks/fills. The separately logged
through-wall health-bar defect is unchanged.

## Automated verification

Coverage includes exact vision boundaries and buildup timing, occlusion and
non-occluding rocks, five-lane scheduling, turn/sweep limits, TrueSight
independence, hiding/reacquisition, personal memory, damage clues, visible-only
projectile dodge, exact search and return timeouts, guard rebasing, pool swaps,
tactical overlays, casting gates, current spell-speed Apply ordering, cache
sharing/invalidation/budgets/disconnected regions, broadphase/brute-force
equivalence, presentation parity, and exact schema-v8 and genuine v2-v7 replay
compatibility. The schema-v7 golden trace was captured from baseline commit
`2727c2e` and remains omniscient.

The perception soak runs 7,200 ticks with 50 living mobs, including 12 engaged
default casters and 38 guarding, hunting, or returning mobs. It exercises map
edits, damage alerts, dodge, retreat, deaths, and restarts while requiring
simulation p99 below 8 ms and heap growth below 64 MiB. A separate particles-off
50-caster case requires zero projectile drops with the default definition and
256-projectile pool.

Run:

```bash
npm run check
npm run test:soak
npm run test:soak:sight
npm run test:soak:spell
npm run test:soak:combat
npm run test:soak:ai
npm run test:soak:perception
git diff --check
```

The browser-only production-adapter fixture is available at
`test/browser/perception_stress.html?renderer=2d` from the development server.
It contains 12 engaged casters and 38 occluded searching guards without adding
a gameplay command or `window.__lantern` mutation API.

## Human release gate

Before the release commit, review Canvas2D, automatic Three.js, forced WebGL 2,
and a trusted-LAN phone. On the reference 1080p desktop with AI View Off,
simulation and presentation CPU p99 must each stay below 8 ms, median frame rate
must reach at least 55 FPS, and frame p95 must be no greater than 22 ms. Exercise
Selected mode separately; All mode has no performance guarantee. Confirm the
facing marker, TrueSight concealment, detection/hiding/search behavior, debug
labels, and resident resource identity. The release commit remains gated on
this behavioral and visual acceptance.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.8.1-playtest-developer-toolbox.md">
# Lantern 0.8.1 Playtest Mode and Developer Toolbox

## Contract and release boundary

Lantern 0.8.1 makes ordinary playtesting the default browser experience. The
application/package/UI version advances to 0.8.1, while snapshot and recording
schema v8, scenario v3, map v1, Fireball definition v1, performance-report v2,
simulation behavior, capacities, command ordering, and replay compatibility all
remain unchanged.

This is a presentation and local-input release. Toggling the toolbox never
injects a fixed-tick command and is not serialized.

## Clean playtest boot

The document boots with `data-mode="play"` and
`data-developer-tools="closed"`. In this state the arena occupies the complete
viewport. The following developer surfaces are absent from layout, hit testing,
keyboard focus, and the accessibility tree:

- the top simulation/edit/focus toolbar and run summary;
- Spell Lab, AI View, and Render Lab windows and launchers;
- the right-side frame, pool, inspector, debug, event, and artifact instruments;
- the bottom pointer/controls rail, corner readout, and authoring palette;
- AI View drawing plus coordinate labels, velocity vectors, particle height
  stems, contact markers, blast-force diagrams, selection inspection, and
  TrueSight diagnostic geometry.

The defeat overlay and ordinary play cursor remain available. Clean mode
suppresses diagnostic drawing at presentation time without changing the
authoritative debug/configuration flags. In particular, recorded ground-bounce
and spark-wall-collision behavior continues exactly as configured.

## Semicolon gate

Pressing the unshifted semicolon key toggles the complete developer toolbox.
The shortcut is ignored while typing in an input, textarea, select, or editable
element. A held key does not repeatedly toggle the toolbox. Opening restores
each developer surface and its existing local state, then requests a renderer
layout refresh for the resized canvas.

Space, period, R, Shift+R, E, and F are developer shortcuts and act only while
the toolbox is open. Mouse movement, casting, camera zoom, and camera pan remain
available in clean playtest mode. Hover inspection and stable-ID pinning are
also toolbox-only so an ordinary cast click cannot leave a hidden diagnostic
selection.

Closing the toolbox closes Render Lab, returns an active scenario editor to
play mode through its existing pause/resume path, and focuses the arena. A
manually paused play session remains paused; reopening the toolbox exposes its
status and Resume control.

## Parked authoring windows

Spell Lab starts closed with a launcher in the toolbox. **Collapse** now removes
the panel from the arena instead of leaving a minimized heading over gameplay;
the launcher reappears in the toolbox. Its draft, validation, applied revision,
seed lock, and last-target state remain in memory. AI View and Render Lab retain
their established read-only/presentation contracts behind the same outer gate.
The non-authoritative direction for applying this launcher-to-window lifecycle
to other qualifying tools is recorded as
[LT-002: Parkable developer windows](../soft-specs/long-term-improvements.md#lt-002-parkable-developer-windows).

## Verification boundary

Automated coverage locks the clean boot attribute, the single developer-surface
CSS gate, full-viewport layout, semicolon recognition, inert/accessibility state
restoration, parked Spell Lab markup, input-shortcut gate, and presentation-only
diagnostic gate. Existing unit, deterministic replay, soak, Canvas2D, and Three.js
tests remain the regression suite. Final visual fit, keyboard feel, and ordinary
playtest acceptance still require a real browser on the target machine.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.8.2-player-follow-camera.md">
# Lantern 0.8.2 Player-Follow Camera

## Contract and release boundary

Lantern 0.8.2 makes the main player the permanent play-camera target in both
Canvas2D and Three.js. The application, package, and UI version advance to
0.8.2. Snapshot and recording schema v8, scenario v3, map v1, Fireball
definition v1, performance-report v2, simulation behavior, commands, and replay
compatibility remain unchanged.

This is a presentation and local-input release. Camera state is neither
authoritative nor serialized, and this release introduces no networking model.

## Exact play-camera attachment

At the beginning of every play-mode render, the camera target resolves from the
same local fixed-tick pose used to draw the player:

`rendered = previous + (current - previous) * alpha`

The bounded runtime alpha is local presentation timing, not network
interpolation. The camera adds no spring, damping, dead zone, look-ahead,
prediction, or persistent offset. This keeps the player's ground anchor fixed
at the viewport center while the world moves smoothly at the display cadence.
The rule remains active while play is running, manually paused, defeated,
resetting, or restarting.

## Input and editor behavior

The stored pointer is reprojected through the camera after every automatic
focus or zoom change and before held-RMB movement commands are sampled. Cursor
markers, visibility gating, casts, selection, inspection, and editor tools
therefore continue to refer to the ground point beneath the visible pointer as
the camera moves.

Wheel zoom in play changes metric coverage around the player without changing
the camera target. MMB panning is ignored in play. Edit mode remains paused and
free: MMB pans in world meters, wheel zoom stays cursor-anchored, and Focus/F
recenters on the displayed player pose. Returning to play reattaches the camera
before the next command or rendered frame.

## Verification boundary

Automated coverage locks render-alpha position resolution, exact Canvas2D and
Three.js camera targets, play/edit ownership, centered and cursor-anchored zoom,
play pan suppression, edit panning, and pointer reprojection. Existing syntax,
simulation, replay, soak, and presentation tests remain the regression suite.

Final acceptance requires real-browser play on Canvas2D, automatic 3D, and
forced WebGL 2. Sustained movement must leave the player ground anchor visually
stationary while the world scrolls smoothly; targeting must remain under the
pointer; edit mode must detach and reattach without a stray play frame. These
perceptual and real-GPU checks cannot be certified by Node tests alone.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.9.0-fireball-investigation-ai.md">
# Lantern 0.9.0 Fireball Investigation AI

## Contract and release boundary

Lantern 0.9.0 advances the application/package/UI plus snapshot and
command-recording schema to v9 and makes `investigative-wizard-v1` the live
enemy profile. Scenario v3, map v1, Fireball definition v1,
performance-report v2, combat rules, pool capacities, and the default
four-alive encounter cadence remain unchanged.

Replay behavior remains selected by recording schema:

- schema v9 records `investigative-wizard-v1`, `enemyCapacity: 64`, and
  `encounterMaximumAlive: 4`;
- schema v8 reconstructs the frozen `perceptive-wizard-v1` schedule and
  behavior exactly;
- schemas v2 through v7 retain their existing legacy profiles and capacities.

This release adds authoritative enemy sensing, not player-facing audio.
Fireball explosions are its only audible stimulus. Footsteps, surface
acoustics, wall attenuation, squad knowledge, caster prediction, networking,
and a general sound-event framework remain deferred.

## Anonymous Fireball evidence

The v9 perception state appends `INVESTIGATING = 5`; `HUNTING` continues to
mean that a previously identified player disappeared. Knowledge sources append
`projectile = 3` and `sound = 4`, preserving every older numeric value.

During the existing staggered 12Hz sample, each enemy considers every visible
hostile Fireball within the existing 12m range, 120-degree cone or 1.5m close
radius, and grid line of sight. The nearest eligible projectile wins, with
ascending effect ID and projectile ID breaking equal-distance ties. A single
sample is sufficient: authoritative position, velocity, and age reconstruct
the launch point as `position - velocity × age`, clamped to a playable
map-cell center. The observation stores effect/projectile identity and pose for
diagnostics, but owner identity never identifies a caster or advances player
exposure.

Every hostile Fireball impact directly tests living enemy wizards within an
inclusive 16m radius. Hearing ignores walls, rocks, darkness, TrueSight, and
presentation state. Allied and neutral effects are ignored. The exact impact
point becomes the anchor, while the existing deterministic search pattern
supplies positional uncertainty. Impact processing follows that tick's AI
decision, so newly heard movement begins on the following tick.

## Priority and state arbitration

All v9 clue paths use one acceptance function. Priority is:

1. current direct sight of the player;
2. seen Fireball trajectory;
3. stored last-seen player position;
4. personal damage;
5. heard Fireball explosion.

Lower-priority evidence cannot redirect or restart stronger behavior. A newer
clue at the same priority redirects; clues received on the same tick use the
unsigned `(effectId, projectileId)` tuple as deterministic freshness. Repeated
samples of one projectile update its diagnostic observation without restarting
travel or search. Its later explosion is recognized as the same effect and
deduplicated before normal priority rejection.

Direct player sight always takes attention. A confirmed player is reacquired
on the first qualifying sample. Anonymous investigation retains the ordinary
15 uninterrupted exposure ticks for first identification; an interrupted
exposure resumes the retained investigation. Player last-seen coordinates may
replace the active anchor without erasing confirmed-target memory. Personal
damage enters `INVESTIGATING` and keeps its immediate local-search behavior.
Dodge and low-health retreat remain movement overrides and preserve the clue.

## Travel, search, and combat boundary

Investigation reuses the v8 navigation and search machinery. A wizard travels
until it reaches 0.75m from the anchor or the 720-tick travel timeout expires,
then searches reachable radius-1 through radius-3 cells for 480 ticks. A search
waypoint changes on arrival or after 90 ticks; the existing scan animation runs
when no waypoint is available. An unsuccessful search returns the wizard to
its guard position and clears the active clue on arrival.

`INVESTIGATING` never enables casting. A shot still requires `ENGAGED`, no
retreat, and a fresh same-tick player visibility check, so launch estimates,
impact points, damage points, and last-seen coordinates are never firing
targets.

## Bounded storage and scheduling

Each of the 64 enemy pool entries owns fixed-capacity investigation source,
priority, anchor, observation/acceptance ticks, effect/projectile IDs, observed
projectile pose/velocity/age, and inferred-origin fields. Spawn, reset, and
swap-move paths initialize and preserve them with the stable actor identity.

Schema v9 performs one map-cell broadphase rebuild before perception and
reuses that build for visual Fireball queries and dodge selection. The existing
post-movement rebuild remains the source for projectile collision and hearing
delivery. Schema-v8 scheduling is untouched. Navigation continues to use the
single preallocated destination-field builder and its global 2,048-expansion
per-tick cap.

## Diagnostics and AI View

Enemy snapshots and `window.__lantern.enemyDiagnostics(id?)` expose an
`investigation` object containing source, priority, anchor, observation and
acceptance ticks, effect/projectile IDs, observed projectile pose/velocity/age,
and inferred origin. `stimulus.position/tick` remains a compatibility view of
the active anchor. A bounded event ring and aggregate counters record
projectile observations, heard explosions, accepted redirects, deduplication,
and priority rejection.

AI View remains read-only. It gives `INVESTIGATING` a distinct color and adds a
sound-impact marker, the selected wizard's 16m hearing radius, projectile
observation point, reverse-trajectory ray, inferred-origin marker, and source,
priority, tick, effect ID, and projectile ID text. The shared overlay remains
presentation-only across Canvas2D and Three.js; TrueSight supplies no AI truth.

## Automated verification

Coverage exercises trajectory reconstruction and clamping, visibility and
team filtering, the inclusive hearing boundary, clue priority/freshness/ties,
same-effect deduplication, behind-wall sound, visible non-threatening
projectiles, trajectory-over-explosion behavior, redirects, last-seen/damage/
sound interactions, exposure and reacquisition, casting prohibition, dodge,
retreat, search/return timing, pool reset/swap, broadphase equivalence, v9
record/replay, and exact schema-v8 and older replay fixtures.

The 7,200-tick perception soak runs 50 living investigative wizards with visual
and auditory Fireball traffic while enforcing the existing p99, heap-growth,
and 2,048-expansion navigation limits. Run:

```bash
npm run check
npm run test:soak:perception
git diff --check
```

## Human release gate

In AI View, verify an occluded wizard hears a hostile explosion and searches
the impact area; a wizard seeing a projectile travels toward its inferred
launch point; that projectile's later explosion does not pull it from the
stronger clue; and travel, scanning, reacquisition, retreat/dodge recovery, and
guard return look natural. Repeat the behavior check in Canvas2D, automatic
Three.js, and forced WebGL 2. Browser/GPU visual acceptance remains separate
from automated simulation and replay evidence.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.9.1-authoring-and-vertical-traversal.md">
# Lantern 0.9.1 Authoring and Vertical Traversal

> **Status:** current application checkpoint
>
> **Application/package:** `0.9.1` · **Snapshot/recording schema:** v12 · **Authoring map:** v5

Lantern 0.9.1 turns the former single-floor debug arena into a practical,
data-driven graybox sandbox without turning the engine into a general 3D
physics or ECS project. It preserves the 0.9.0 Fireball-investigation contract
and adds the authored world and limited vertical traversal needed to begin
building a small temple map.

## What is now current

- The saved source is a versioned `lantern-authoring-map` v5 document with
  stable IDs, named layers, signed base heights, surface and structure grids,
  sparse instances, player-start ownership, holes, and map-level connectors.
- The generated editor palette, deterministic picking, inspector, previews,
  semantic undo/redo, dirty state, validation, atomic load/import, and one
  optional reference layer all operate on authored source rather than runtime
  pools or renderer objects.
- Compilation is deterministic and per-layer. It derives compact collision and
  occlusion masks plus bounded runtime spawn recipes; runtime pushing, falling,
  elevator travel, and torch lights never rewrite authored starts.
- Eligible player, enemy, and dynamic clutter bodies have bounded world-Y,
  vertical velocity, support, and layer state. Floors and elevators are support
  providers, not passenger containers.
- A two-stop elevator follows an authored, unstoppable clock: default one-second
  dwell and two-second legs. It carries fitting riders without centering or
  locking X/Z, rejects oversized upper-frame loads deterministically, and keeps
  torch-light identity attached to its live body.
- Single-cell holes use the same positive-clearance aperture contract as upper
  elevator shafts. Fitting bodies receive rim attraction, can fall through
  multiple aligned floors, and can steer to land on an intermediate frame.
- `Space` starts one supported, committed player jump toward the world target.
  It can clear eligible low clutter and holes but has no double jump, variable
  height, or free jump steering. Pressure plates are visible authored instances
  and react only to eligible bodies genuinely supported on their floor.

## Compatibility boundary

Schema v12 records the committed jump command and current authoring-map
projection. Schema v11 and older recordings retain their frozen commands and
simulation branches. Authoring-map v4/v3/v2/v1 and legacy map/scenario imports
migrate explicitly to v5; successful saves emit v5. Scenario v3 remains the
compiled compatibility projection for recordings. Fireball definition v1 and
the 0.9.0 investigative-wizard behavior are unchanged.

## Test and explore

Run `npm run check` for the syntax and full Node test suite. Then start the
browser with `npm start -- --port 4174` if the default port is busy. Use
`?arena=holes&renderer=3d` for the integrated traversal playground: four floors,
staggered lifts, aligned holes, an intermediate landing, jump/plate fixtures, a
fitting torch or rock, and an oversized table.

Open the developer toolbox with `;`; `E` enters authoring, `Space` jumps, `P`
pauses, and `.` advances one fixed tick. Browser/GPU visual acceptance remains a
human check: verify deck/shaft visuals, floor handoff continuity, torch-light
continuity, controls, and the intended authored layout on the chosen renderer.

## Explicitly deferred

This checkpoint does not add a general 3D rigid-body system, arbitrary holes,
walking on clutter, fall damage, elevators called or navigated by AI, cross-layer
navigation/sight/sound/projectiles, doors, trap wiring, prefab rooms, persistent
history, autosave, map streaming, networking, final art, or authoritative
lighting.
</file>

<file path="docs/milestones/0.9.2-elevator-spell-impacts.md">
# Lantern 0.9.2 Elevator Spell Impacts

> **Status:** current application checkpoint
>
> **Application/package:** `0.9.2` · **Snapshot/recording schema:** v13 · **Authoring map:** v5

Lantern 0.9.2 makes elevator geometry a first-class Fireball blocker without
changing elevator movement, rider support, or dynamic-body collision.

## Current behavior

- A Fireball on a lower elevator layer explodes on the square deck while the
  lift dwells at its lower stop, and on the circular piston/shaft while the
  lift travels or dwells at its upper stop.
